  ],
  "text": "Almost nobody ever travelled the Silk Road. The goods did. The people mostly did not. You picture one caravan crossing a continent. That almost never happened. A trader worked one segment. Buy at this end, sell at that end, turn around, go home. The next trader picked it up and carried it one more stretch. Which is why the price multiplied. Every handover was somebody else's margin. And it is why nobody could control it. There was no single road to close. Silk went west. So did paper, gunpowder and mathematics. Religions travelled it too, changing shape at every handover, because nobody carried the whole story. So did the Black Death. Same routes, same relay, same speed. It was never a road. It was a chain of short trips that happened to reach across the world. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
